{% comment %} Task 18 (§7.4): was layouts/base.liquid, which skips the rail/nav
   AND the skip link (default.liquid:10-46) entirely — the six post-type
   permalink pages built through this layout had no skip link and no
   id="skiplink-content" target at all, only the homepage did. Routing
   through default.liquid instead of duplicating the skip link/main markup
   here fixes it once for every post type, matching how page.liquid already
   does it. default.liquid adds no wrapper around {{ content }} besides
   <main>, so the h-entry markup below is unaffected. {% endcomment %}
{%- comment %} This render is the whole point of the page (unlike
   post-list-entry.liquid, which renders the same type partials in a loop
   further down the homepage) — its own image, if it has one, is the page's
   LCP candidate. See types/photo.liquid. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign lcp = true -%}
{%- comment %} Task S3 (audit §2.3): <h1 class="p-name primary-title"> lives
   here, not in types/article.liquid — that partial is shared with
   post-list-entry.liquid's feed loop, and a heading baked into it produced a
   second homepage <h1> and zero on the five titleless permalink types. It
   has to sit *inside* <article class="h-entry"> to count as that entry's
   p-name to a real mf2 parser (the exact class of bug already fixed once
   for u-url, see post-list-entry.liquid's own comment) — so the article/
   h-entry wrapper moves here too, and the type partials render body-only
   (mirrors the same pattern already used, unreachably, by layouts/
   page.liquid). Each type partial's own <a class="u-url"> still supplies
   p-name for the feed-listing render, where this <h1> never appears — see
   types/article.liquid for how it tells the two contexts apart. {% endcomment -%}
{%- comment %} Task (visual fix): source's own post.liquid:7 —
`<h1 class="p-name primary-title{% if smalltitle %} primary-title-smalltitle{% endif %}">`
— is the scoping mechanism for `--fluid-type` (theme-typography.scss:192-199,
source _theme.scss:600-607): `.primary-title` gets the 68-120px hero clamp by
default, `.primary-title-smalltitle` (added alongside it) overrides to the
44-90px clamp. That conditional class never existed in this port at all —
restored verbatim here, reading an optional `smalltitle` front-matter flag,
same opt-in as source (checked: only 2 of the source's 471 real posts set
it, both unusually long titles — "DOMContentLoaded Inconsistencies (in
Browsers and JavaScript Libraries)", "Selecting XML Nodes with JavaScript, or
the Peril of getElementsByTagName" — so the *default*, no-flag case is the
large clamp, matching typical short titles like "Checklist for Web
Applications" in the source's own corpus. A short fixture title like "An
example article" falls in that same default bucket in source too — the
large rendering is faithful, not a leftover scoping bug; see
task-visual-fix-report.md for the full evidence trail. {%- endcomment -%}
<article class="{% if postType == "event" %}h-event{% else %}h-entry{% endif %}">
  {%- comment %} Indiekit adaptation: the source opts INTO the smaller clamp
   per post (`smalltitle: true` in front matter, e.g. _posts/2008-05-10-*.md).
   Indiekit titles are written by a Micropub client, so no author will ever set
   that flag — and the 68-120px hero clamp renders a normal-length post title
   across three lines. Default to the smaller clamp and let a post opt OUT with
   `hugeTitle: true` if it ever wants the hero size. {% endcomment -%}
  {%- comment %} Four of the six Indiekit post types are titleless by design.
   _data/eleventyComputed.js synthesises a "<Type> · <date>" title for them so
   the Atom feed has a non-empty <title> — but rendering that as a hero heading
   puts "PHOTO · 30 AUG 2026" at the top of a photo post, which is noise.
   The source already has this mechanism: page.liquid adds `sr-only` to its own
   <h1> when `removeTitle` is set. Apply it to the synthetic case, so the
   heading stays in the document for microformats and screen readers and stops
   dominating the page. {% endcomment -%}
    {%- comment %} p-name is conditional, not decorative. In mf2 a note is
   DEFINED by the absence of an explicit p-name: parsers imply the name from
   the content, and consumers compare name against content to decide whether
   to render a post as a titled article or as a note. eleventyComputed.js
   synthesises a "<Type> · <date>" label for the four titleless types so the
   Atom feed's atom:title is never empty — but marking that synthetic label
   up as p-name made every note, like, reply and photo parse as a titled
   article named after its own timestamp. The Atom requirement and the mf2
   requirement are satisfied in different places: `title` still carries the
   label into the feed, this class does not carry it into the markup.
   title_isReal is stamped from RAW front matter by the "real-title"
   preprocessor (eleventy.config.js), before the synthesis runs. {% endcomment -%}
    <h1 class="{% if title_isReal %}p-name {% endif %}primary-title{% unless hugeTitle %} primary-title-smalltitle{% endunless %}{% unless title_isReal %} sr-only{% endunless %}">{{ title }}</h1>
  {% include "partials/author-card.liquid" %}
  {% include "partials/entry-meta.liquid" %}
  {% include "partials/syndication-links.liquid" %}
  {% case postType %}
    {% when "article" %}{% include "types/article.liquid" %}
    {% when "bookmark" %}{% include "types/bookmark.liquid" %}
    {% when "like" %}{% include "types/like.liquid" %}
    {% when "photo" %}{% include "types/photo.liquid" %}
    {% when "reply" %}{% include "types/reply.liquid" %}
    {% when "audio" %}{% include "types/audio.liquid" %}
    {% when "video" %}{% include "types/video.liquid" %}
    {% when "event" %}{% include "types/event.liquid" %}
    {% when "jam" %}{% include "types/jam.liquid" %}
    {% when "repost" %}{% include "types/repost.liquid" %}
    {% when "rsvp" %}{% include "types/rsvp.liquid" %}
    {% else %}{% include "types/note.liquid" %}
  {% endcase %}
  {%- comment %} Categories on the PERMALINK, not just the listing row. The
     .tags slot each type partial reserves lives in its feed-listing branch
     only, so without this a post's p-category existed on the homepage and
     vanished on the page a consumer actually fetches. .tags:empty
     (_theme-chrome.scss:45) hides it for posts with no categories. {% endcomment -%}
  {% include "partials/post-tags.liquid" %}
</article>
{%- comment %} schema.org JSON-LD, one type per post type. Sits outside the
   h-entry: microformats2 is the canonical machine-readable layer here and
   this is a second, independent representation for search engines — nesting
   it inside the entry would imply a relationship that does not exist.
{% endcomment -%}
{%- if jsonLdData %}
<script type="application/ld+json">{{ jsonLdData | jsonLdScript: content }}</script>
{%- endif %}

{%- comment %} Finding 11: this port shipped the source's whole dl.nextprev
   design (_lists.scss:33-108, 18 rules on every page) without ever building the
   feature — readers could not move between posts and paid for the dead bytes.

   Adapted, not copied, in two ways:

   1. ORDER. The source reads collections.activePosts, which it keeps
      newest-first, so ITS "next" is the older post. collections.post here is
      Eleventy's default ascending order (content/archive.liquid reverses it to
      display newest-first), so getNextCollectionItem returns the NEWER post.
      Copying the source's labels verbatim would have pointed both arrows the
      wrong way. Bound to meaning here — olderPost/newerPost — and then placed
      into whichever class carries the correct column, since .nextprev-next is
      grid-column 1 (left) and .nextprev-prev is grid-column 2 (right).
   2. NO THUMBNAILS. The source's cells are OG-image screenshots via its
      {% ogImageSource %} shortcode. Four of six Indiekit post types are
      titleless, and generating a per-neighbour image on every permalink is
      build cost for a nav affordance — the post's title (real, or
      eleventyComputed.js's "<Type> · <date>" label, which reads correctly AS a
      nav label) is the link text instead. dl.nextprev dd:empty already styles
      the empty cell for the first and last post. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign olderPost = collections.post | getPreviousCollectionItem: page -%}
{%- assign newerPost = collections.post | getNextCollectionItem: page -%}
{%- if olderPost or newerPost %}
<nav class="content-grid-sibling" data-pagefind-ignore aria-label="More posts">
	<dl class="nextprev nextprev-text">
		<dt class="nextprev-next">{% if olderPost %}<span>&lt; Older</span>{% endif %}</dt>
		<dd class="nextprev-next">{% if olderPost %}<a href="{{ olderPost.url }}">{{ olderPost.data.title }}</a>{% endif %}</dd>
		<dt class="nextprev-prev">{% if newerPost %}<span>Newer &gt;</span>{% endif %}</dt>
		<dd class="nextprev-prev">{% if newerPost %}<a href="{{ newerPost.url }}">{{ newerPost.data.title }}</a>{% endif %}</dd>
	</dl>
</nav>
{%- endif %}

{%- capture absoluteUrl %}{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}{% endcapture -%}
{% include "webmentionlist.liquid" %}
